WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You served with distinction,
won a couple of medals

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:06.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
for your service, but you also
were a local celebrity.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
You had a show,
a television show in Iraq,

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:09.909 line:92%
called "Moulton and Mohammed."

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:12.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Moulton and Mohammed"
with my translator, Mohammed.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
And -- I mean,
we were celebrities.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:16.750 line:92%
I mean, I used to get fan mail.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.852
I used to have to sign
autographs in the street.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.920 size:81% position:41%
I mean, no one asks me
for my autograph now

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a member of Congress.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, this is a total step down
in terms of --

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.459
-And so here -- so here are you
and your translator.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's Mohammad over there.
So what was the show?

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:30.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, what did you guys --
you were --

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:32.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
What was the goal of
broadcasting in Iraq to people

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.502
who had obviously never had
non-state-controlled television?

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:38.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it was amazing
because we actually believed

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:39.172 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in the Marine Corps

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a free media
was important to a democracy.

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:43.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
Sort of a crazy concept
these days.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:48.381 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But -- But the first thing we
had to do is

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
we just had to get
something on TV,

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
because all this TV station
had ever done

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.588
was play tapes from the Ministry
of Information in Baghdad.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.291
So the first thing we did
was we got a DVD player,

00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:00.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
hooked it up
to their transmitter,

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:01.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
and these Iraqis
used to put in movies --

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
American movies
they would get from the market

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
and watch them
for the first time

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
while simultaneously
broadcasting them

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.403 size:75% position:38%
to million of Iraqis
across the country.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.871 line:92%
And the mistake we made is

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
we forgot to
explain the rating system.

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:15.141 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.377
-So what would happen is they'd
get to a racy section

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:20.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
of an American film,
and they'd quickly hit eject.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
They had this 10-volume set of
Islamic history videos

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:26.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they would put in to sort
of calm everybody down.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:27.353 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:29.556
But one night they didn't switch
quickly enough.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:30.723 line:92%
The movie was "Basic Instinct."

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, that's one you want to
switch, yeah.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.627 size:78% position:39%
-And so the next day,
there were protests.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.363
I mean, men throwing rocks,
women wailing in the streets.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mohammed, my translator and I,
just 'cause we were sort of

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
in charge of the TV station,
we got death threats.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.205
At the end of a very long day,
I turned to Mohammad, I said,

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:48.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Let's just go -- let's go get
tea with the Iraqi police."

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause these are guys in their
20s and 30s.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, surely they wouldn't
be so offended

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:53.213 line:92%
by what was on TV last night.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:56.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was a great
cultural lesson for me

00:01:56.015 --> 00:02:00.253
because I walked into this room
full of young Iraqi men,

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.555 line:92%
and they were immediately irate.

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:05.959
They were yelling and screaming
at me, and I turned to Mohammed

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.528
and I said, "Mohammed, what are
they saying?"

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he said,
"Sir, they say every time

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:11.698 size:81% position:41% line:92%
you put a movie on TV,

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:15.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
you turn it off right
when we get to the good part."

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.003 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:21.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So a lot -- a lot of
similarities with 20-year-olds

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.976 line:92%
here in the States as well.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:26.279
One of the things you are hoping
to do is recruit

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:29.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
a new generation of people
in public service

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.950 line:92%
that come from the military.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.889
Veterans can have great purpose
here serving in office.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:38.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
What is it about veterans
that you think

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.560 align:start size:94% position:6%
is important
for our political system?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.229
-Well, look, I wouldn't be here
if not for being a veteran.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:45.598 size:88% position:44%
I mean, I didn't grow up
interested in politics

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:46.299 size:81% position:41% line:92%
or anything like that.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was actually recruited by
a non-profit

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
trying to get veterans to run
for office at a time

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we've never had fewer
veterans in Congress

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:55.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
in our nation's history
than we do today.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:57.777 line:92%
And when I decided to run,

00:02:57.777 --> 00:03:01.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was because the Congressman
in my home district,

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
Congressman Bates' successor.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.250 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He managed to pass one bill
in 18 years.

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.020 line:92%
He was incredibly partisan.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.423
And I was like, look, if no one
is willing to stand up to him,

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
then the system
is never going to change.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the problem is,
I was so naive about politics,

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't know that you're not
supposed to take on

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.832 size:75% position:38%
an 18-year incumbent
in your own party.

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.736
And so the whole national
Democratic establishment

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:24.337 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
was against me.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
They said,
"You're going to lose."

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:27.106 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And by the way,

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.375
you will never run for anything
again in politics,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you don't challenge
the establishment.

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:35.215
And fundamentally what they were
saying to me as a veteran was

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:37.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
do not participate
in the democracy

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.986 line:92%
you risked your life to defend.

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And that's wrong.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.790
-And have you -- So you had
this interesting situation,

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.293
which is now you're in this very
partisan Washington D.C.,

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:49.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
but kind of
your first political battle

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:50.964 line:92%
was against your own party.

00:03:50.964 --> 00:03:54.234 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like that puts you
in a better situation

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
to deal with all of
these different --

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:59.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Look, the party establishment
has gotten us

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
into a terrible position
right now.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.576 size:88% position:44%
I mean, we don't control
anything in Washington,

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:07.280
and so I think it's time for
a new generation of Democrats

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.081 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
to step up and run.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.382 line:92%
And the thing about veterans is

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:11.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
they know how to
challenge the establishment.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.587
They're not afraid to take on,
you know, the people in control.

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.290
They're not afraid to put people
over politics

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.157 line:92%
or over their political party,

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:23.062
and I've got 19 unbelievable
candidates that I've endorsed

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
and encouraged to run
and everything.

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:27.533
People like Mikie Sherrill
right down in New Jersey,

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.901 line:92%
who's taking on the chairman of

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:30.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
the House
Appropriations Committee.

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:33.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean this -- The chairman of
the House Appropriations

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
has more money
flowing under his pen

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
than any politician
in the world,

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:38.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she just scared him
out of the race

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:40.346
because she's such an incredible
leader.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:41.481 line:92%
She's a Navy helicopter pilot.

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's really --
-She's going to win.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.484 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:51.524
And I think that, you know,
the problem right now is

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:54.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've got to look ourselves in
the mirror as Democrats

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.396 line:92%
and recognize that we've lost.

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:58.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, you know,
we stand for the right things,

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:00.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we've lost a lot of
elections,

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
and unless we're willing
to change,

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
unless we're willing to make
some difference,

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:05.738 line:92%
unless we're willing to stand up

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
and talk about where we think
we should take America,

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
rather than just
bashing the Republicans,

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:12.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
let's talk about our vision
for America.

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.681
Let's talk about how we're going
to tackle the economy

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:15.381 size:78% position:39% line:92%
and national defense.

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:16.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's talk about what we're
going to do,

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:17.617 align:start size:94% position:6%
not just what Trump
is doing wrong.

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.185
-I think one of the things
that Trump did, obviously,

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:23.222
is he had these very bold policy
proposals in the 2016 election.

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:26.392
Now, whether or not any of them
were rational,

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:27.093 line:92%
they were certainly bold.

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:31.030
What are -- Do you think in the
next, not only 2018, but 2020,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
what are the bold
policy proposals

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:34.167 line:92%
that Democrats need to take?

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:37.603
-Look, one of the things we have
to do is tackle the economy.

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:40.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the reality is a lot of
people in America

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
are getting left out
of the economy.

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:43.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not because of what
Trump says.

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:45.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's not because immigrants
are taking jobs.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.980
It's because they're being
automated out of existence.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, literally,
robots are taking jobs.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:52.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
So rather than
take Trump's strategy of

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:55.655
let's go back into the coal
mines and pretend it's 1955,

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:57.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
let's actually embrace
the new economy

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
and talk about how
everybody in America,

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:00.927 line:92%
no matter where you live,

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:03.930
has a meaningful role to play in
the economy of the future.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
Let's talk about how we can
actually expand

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
healthcare access
to everybody in America

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:09.268 line:92%
rather than quibble about

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:11.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
how many people are going to
lose healthcare.

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:14.874
You know, let's talk about
what a Democratic vision

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:15.608 size:84% position:42% line:92%
of national defense is,

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:19.145
where we build alliances across
the globe and promote peace,

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:23.249
rather than try to start wars
with North Korea. I mean --

00:06:23.249 --> 00:06:24.450 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I -- That all sounds
very good to me.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.657
I hope that everybody else wants
to hear that message as well.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:33.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much for making
the time to come here.

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:34.694 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-Hey, thank you, Seth.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:36.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thanks for taking care of
Massachusetts' 6th.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
Congressman Seth Moulton,
everybody.

